<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seleucus I Nicator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 358–<a href="/years/281-bce">281 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Seleucus I, Seleucus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seleucus I Nicator was a Macedonian general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. After Alexander's death he secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and built a realm stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He founded many cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>, and his recovery of Babylon became the starting point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-era">Seleucid era</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator.jpg" alt="Seleucus I Nicator" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allan Gluck · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seleukos_I_Nikator_Bronze_Roman_100BCE-100CE_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_Naples_AN_5590_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Seleucus was the son of Antiochus, who may have belonged to an upper Macedonian noble family, and was born at Europus in northern Macedonia. His year of birth is unclear. Ancient writers give his age at the Battle of Corupedium as 77, 73 or 75, which would place his birth in 358, 354 or 356 BCE; the last date would make him the same age as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, which is most likely propaganda on his part. In spring 334 BCE, aged about twenty-three, he accompanied Alexander into Asia, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Indian campaigns</a> he commanded the elite infantry corps of the Hypaspistai, and fought against the elephants of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> at the Hydaspes in 326 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Alexander died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> on 10 June 323 BCE, Seleucus was made commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> and chiliarch under the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>. Perdiccas was assassinated in a conspiracy that included Seleucus, though his participation has not been established, and at Triparadisus in 321 BCE Seleucus received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of Babylon. In about 315 BCE he quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he served Ptolemy as an admiral. He returned to Babylon in 312 BCE with Ptolemy's support, defeated Nicanor, the satrap of Media, and over the following years brought the eastern part of Alexander's empire as far as the Jaxartes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> rivers under his authority.</p><p>In 306 BCE he took the title of king, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, Ptolemy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>. His war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> in 305 to 303 BCE ended in a treaty by which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> annexed the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, and Seleucus received 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. They decided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE, where he, Cassander and Lysimachus defeated Antigonus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> passed to Seleucus. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 300 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, which overshadowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. In 281 BCE he defeated Lysimachus at Corupedium and added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to his empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Seleucus then crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, intending to leave Asia to his son Antiochus I, whom he had named co-ruler in 292 BCE. Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a> in September 281 BCE he was assassinated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos">Ptolemy Ceraunus</a>, who had taken refuge at his court. Antiochus succeeded him and founded a cult of his father, and Seleucus was later worshipped as a son of Zeus Nikator. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> remained one of the major powers of the Hellenistic world until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> overcame it in the late second and early first centuries BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seleucus I Nicator” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 280 BCE, while the Macedonian kingdoms were in turmoil after the deaths of Lysimachus and <b>Seleucus I</b>, four western Achaean cities, Dyme, Patrae, Tritaia and Pharai, renewed the league.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 305 BCE <b>Seleucus I</b> ceded his eastern territories to Chandragupta Maurya for 500 war elephants and a marriage agreement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In a war and treaty with <b>Seleucus I Nicator</b> around 305–303 BCE, he gained Arachosia, Gedrosia and the Paropamisadae, covering much of modern Pakistan and parts of Afghanistan, in return for 500 war elephants, and received the Greek envoy Megasthenes at his court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Seleucus I</b> traded eastern lands to Chandragupta Maurya for 500 war elephants, which helped him win the Battle of Ipsus in 301 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>He was an ally of Lysimachus, <b>Seleucus</b> and Ptolemy against Antigonus I.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Seleucus I</b> defeated and killed him at Corupedium in 281 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lysimachus">Kingdom of Lysimachus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Europos, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Lysimachia, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.89,22.55&amp;year=-308" aria-label="Europos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5102 704 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Seleucus I Nicator"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5552 974h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5552 974h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.89,22.55&amp;year=-308">What was here in 308 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
